  "notes": "The current commercial centre of Memphis, and the only entry in this batch whose harmony is not written but *inherited from a sample*. The research is specific: the chords come from 80s R&B flips — \"Wockesha\" is built on DeBarge's \"Stay with Me\" — giving genuine ii–v–i and minor-9 colour, and his stated workflow is sample-first (\"we were just going through a lot of samples\"). So `extensions` inverts the parent almost completely (48% sevenths, 22% ninths), `maxChords` reaches 4, `topVoiceStepwise` comes back on, and the sub **follows the sample's bass motion rather than pedalling** — `bassline.rhythm` is `independent_riff` at `followRootsProb` 0.60 with the longest portamento in the batch (70–150 ms) and a 20-semitone register. The second described inversion is the mix: **the kit is mixed behind the sample so the loop reads as the lead**, which is why `velocityTiers` are the quietest here while the hats are among the busiest — dense triplet rolls with 32nd flams, so the roll vocab weights the triplet 5:2. His own stated method — \"let the beat breathe\", leave bars of loop with no vocal — is `emptyBarBias` 0.26 over 4- and 8-bar phrases. ⚠ `medium`: the research rates it high-medium — the sample source, the producer bench, the chart facts and his own account of the workflow are sourced, but **the tempo band is inferred** (65–78 half-time = 130–156 straight, marked [LOW-CONF]), so `session.bpm` is the one parameter here not traceable to a described element. ⚠ The no-drums sample intro the research describes is already how the inherited `arrangement.sectionRules.intro` behaves (melody only), so it is not re-authored; only `sectionBars.intro` widens to 4–8. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 46 to 43 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 44 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 3.",
